  "response": "**Verdict: Request changes, do not block.**\n\nThe finding (`crime_00012`) reports the asset at **260,000 bytes (253.9 KB)** — just over the low threshold (≥200 KB), well under medium (≥500 KB) and high (≥1000 KB). For a landing-page hero this is a real LCP/Core Web Vitals concern, but it's not catastrophic — fix-on-the-PR, not a merge blocker.\n\n**Specific transformation:**\n\n1. **Target dimensions.** A landing-page hero typically renders at viewport width on desktop. Ship a responsive `srcset`:\n   - `~1600w` (desktop/2x of common 800px render slot)\n   - `~1024w` (tablet)\n   - `~640w` (mobile)\n   \n   Whatever the visible render box is in the layout (likely ~800–1200 CSS px tall × full-width), the largest variant should be ≤2× that in physical pixels. A single 1600×800 hero is usually plenty.\n\n2. **Format: AVIF as primary, WebP as fallback.**\n   - **AVIF** for the primary `<source>`: photographic hero content is exactly where AVIF wins — typically 30–50% smaller than WebP at equivalent visual quality. Browser support is now ~95% globally (Chrome, Firefox, Safari 16+, Edge).\n   - **WebP** as the `<source>` fallback for the ~5% (mainly older Safari) that still lacks AVIF.\n   - Keep PNG as the final `<img src>` fallback only if you care about ancient browsers; otherwise drop it.\n\n   Use a `<picture>` element so the browser picks one:\n   ```html\n   <picture>\n     <source type=\"image/avif\" srcset=\"hero-640.avif 640w, hero-1024.avif 1024w, hero-1600.avif 1600w\" />\n     <source type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"hero-640.webp 640w, hero-1024.webp 1024w, hero-1600.webp 1600w\" />\n     <img src=\"hero-1024.webp\" alt=\"…\" width=\"1600\" height=\"800\" loading=\"eager\" fetchpriority=\"high\" />\n   </picture>\n   ```\n\nRealistic outcome: a 1600w AVIF of a typical hero photo lands in the 40–80 KB range — roughly 3–6× smaller than the current PNG, and the responsive smaller variants help mobile LCP more than the desktop reduction does.",
